-- title:
--   The selected-color scale bounds every principal encoding dimension
-- statement:
--   For a natural input size $n$, put $m=\operatorname{log2}(n)$, $h=\lfloor\lfloor\sqrt[3]{m}\rfloor/2\rfloor$, $J=20\lfloor m/(200h)\rfloor$, and $M=2^{2048(m+1)}$. Here $\operatorname{log2}(0)=0$ and natural division by zero is zero. Let $p$ be the selected local prime for $h$, set $P=2^{\lfloor\sqrt m\rfloor+1}$, and define the selected-color scale
--   $$S=M+2^{5000000(h+1)^2}+p^J(4P)^2+P+n+100.$$
--   Let $g=\left\lceil\left\lceil\sqrt{\binom{512h}{2h}^{J}}\right\rceil/2^{\lfloor2hJ/20\rfloor}\right\rceil$ be the prescribed band size.
--   Writing $W=512hJ$ and $\mu=2hJ$, one has
--   $$W+1\le S,\qquad J+1\le S,\qquad \mu+1\le S,\qquad g\le S,\qquad p^J\le S.$$
--
--   These simultaneous inequalities bound the width, block count, source cutoff, band size, and radix by the same scale used for selected-color resources.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/c0ldzy17/three-sum-lean/blob/2b639655e07421b9ac743de9090459bab473a7c0/Submission/ThreeSum/EncodedColorResourceScale.lean#L37-L51

